// C80 / E-Report: an unguarded zero divisor CONTINUES and logs a Warn — it never
// traps, so the spreadsheet model still degrades one value and lets the rest of the
// program compute.  The VALUE is IEEE, and NaN is the one float null (`doc/23-safety`:
// "NaN (Not a Number) is null"): `0.0 / 0.0` is null, `1.0 / 0.0` is `inf` — a
// representable value, not a missing one.
//
// Forcing NaN here instead (loft#983) made ONE expression answer two things: `inf`
// inline, where the `*Nullable` peer is emitted, and `null` once bound or returned.
// It also split division from its own sibling — float OVERFLOW (`1.0e308 * 10.0`)
// keeps `inf` in every position — though both produce the identical IEEE value.  And
// it made `??` self-defeating: `a / b ?? 0.0`, the idiom every numeric library uses to
// defend a divide, selected the peer that never yields null, so the guard guarded
// nothing (`mesh3d::normalize3` was sound only by accident).
//
// The `*Nullable` peers are now behaviourally identical to these and are dead weight —
// a separable cleanup, exactly as the integer split already is (formal/ROADMAP D-op-4).
fn OpDivFloat(v1: float, v2: float) -> float;
#rust"{{ if @v2 == 0.0 {{ s.raise_recoverable(crate::runtime_error::RuntimeErrorKind::DivideByZero); }} @v1 / @v2 }}"
