// Same fresh-store leg: the callee allocated its own store, ignored the placeholder, so the
// orphan is freed here.  The ADOPTION leg differs, and that is the whole point — when the two
// name one store the callee is handing back the store it minted, and a caller that borrows the
// result will never free it.  Nobody would.  So the store is handed to the frame that will
// HOLD it, by the rule `State::release_fnref_bufs` already applies to a delivered return
// buffer: owned by possession, released when that frame ends.
//
// Emitted only where the caller's reading is known to be a borrow — a lambda whose published
// return names its `__closure` — so the adoption leg cannot collide with a caller that frees
// the store itself.  See `formal/closures.md` D-clo-13 and @PLN150.
